$ enola hook --help
exit: 0
--- stdout
--- stderr
Usage: enola hook <session-start|stop>

Run by your coding agent, not by you: `enola install --hooks` registers it.
Each event reads the agent's JSON payload on stdin.

  session-start  pin a baseline of the session's repository, in a detached
                 process, so the session's start never waits on a snapshot
  stop           grade what the session changed against that baseline and,
                 only when it introduced a regression, report it back to the
                 agent

It never breaks, delays or clutters a session: every failure, and every
event this build does not know, exits 0 and says nothing. Run
`enola doctor` to see whether the hooks are firing.

Examples:
  # Register the hooks with your agents
  enola install --hooks

  # See whether they are firing
  enola doctor

See also: enola help install, enola help doctor
